415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Buddhism originally did not have a tantric tradition but in the early part of the first millenium BC it developed one, that was after direct contact with the teachings of ancient Egypt.....There's a strong connection between Memphite Theology \(Memphisism\) and early Buddhism, it is reasonable to say that Buddhism and its creators were impelled by ancient Egyptian religion just as were the Confucius, the Pythagoreans, and the Taoists.\(Ashby,The Ancient Eygptian Origins of Buddhism, 2006\))
/E (Buddhism originally did not have a tantric tradition but in the early part of the first millenium BC it developed one, that was after direct contact with the teachings of ancient Egypt.....There's a strong connection between Memphite Theology \(Memphisism\) and early Buddhism, it is reasonable to say that Buddhism and its creators were impelled by ancient Egyptian religion just as were the Confucius, the Pythagoreans, and the Taoists.\(Ashby,The Ancient Eygptian Origins of Buddhism, 2006\))
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T ("It is of course possible that a complex diffusion situation obtained parts of the doctrine descending into both Greece and India from some earlier source, or other parts being passed from one of these cultures to another at a later time. But what is clear and what should enter the general discussion on the topic, is that the tantric physiology is not exclusively an Asian element and that a diffusion situation probably involving some of these factors just reviewed was involved in its presence in India as well as Greece but there may be a still more ancient world involved." \(Harper, K; Brown, R. The Roots of Tantra,2002.... Paper by: Thomas Evilley, The Art History and Archaeology of Tantra; The Spinal Serpent.\))
/E ("It is of course possible that a complex diffusion situation obtained parts of the doctrine descending into both Greece and India from some earlier source, or other parts being passed from one of these cultures to another at a later time. But what is clear and what should enter the general discussion on the topic, is that the tantric physiology is not exclusively an Asian element and that a diffusion situation probably involving some of these factors just reviewed was involved in its presence in India as well as Greece but there may be a still more ancient world involved." \(Harper, K; Brown, R. The Roots of Tantra,2002.... Paper by: Thomas Evilley, The Art History and Archaeology of Tantra; The Spinal Serpent.\))
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
